**Part 1: restyle the page.**
- Read what must change (background and text colors, a button's style or text) and find the elements in the HTML: ids, classes and tags.
- Edit the CSS (or add a rule) targeting those elements:

```css
body { background-color: #f5f7fa; color: #222; }
.btn-primary {
  background-color: #0a66c2; color: #fff;
  border: none; border-radius: 6px; padding: 10px 20px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
.btn-primary:hover { background-color: #084d92; }
```

- If your rule is ignored, check **specificity** and order: an id beats a class, a class beats a tag, and inline styles beat all of them. A later rule wins at equal specificity. Use the browser's inspector to see which rule applies.
- Keep ids, class names and structure unchanged, because the checker may look for them. Use the exact color values asked for.

**Part 2: join three tables.** Assume `students(id, name)`, `enrollments(student_id, course_id)`, `courses(id, title)`.

```sql
SELECT s.name, c.title
FROM students s
JOIN enrollments e ON e.student_id = s.id
JOIN courses c ON c.id = e.course_id
ORDER BY s.name;
```

The middle (linking) table holds both foreign keys, so you join the first table to it and then it to the third. Each `JOIN ... ON` needs its own condition. Use `LEFT JOIN` if students without courses must also appear. Add `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` and `HAVING` as the question requires, and match the requested column names and order exactly.

**Common mistakes.** Missing an `ON` condition (a cross product with many rows), ambiguous column names, and editing the wrong stylesheet.
